# =============================================================
# AfamFresh - block non-servable files
# =============================================================
# The app lives in a subdirectory of htdocs with no vhost, so every
# file under it is reachable over HTTP by default. Verified: a .yaml
# file placed here is returned with HTTP 200 and its contents in
# plain text. Everything below closes that off.
#
# This depends on AllowOverride being enabled (it is under XAMPP's
# default htdocs config). On a server where it is not — or on nginx —
# these rules are silently ignored, so keep env.yaml outside the
# document root there and point AFAMFRESH_ENV_FILE at it.
# =============================================================

# Anything named env.* — the credentials file and every variant of it.
# Matching the prefix rather than the extension is deliberate: editors
# leave env.yaml~, env.yaml.save and env.yaml.bak behind, and an
# extension rule sails straight past all three while they sit in the
# web root containing the same secrets.
<FilesMatch "^env\.">
    Require all denied
</FilesMatch>

# Credentials, dumps, backups, and anything else that is data rather
# than a page. .bak/.sql matter because working copies of config and
# database dumps have a way of ending up next to the code.
<FilesMatch "\.(yaml|yml|env|bak|sql|ini|log|save|swp|orig)$|~$">
    Require all denied
</FilesMatch>

# Source files that are not pages. An ApiService.kt copied into api/ was
# served with HTTP 200 and handed the whole endpoint list to anyone who
# guessed the name; nothing here is meant to be fetched, and the only
# non-PHP file this app still serves is
# .well-known/assetlinks.json (Android App Links verification — Google's
# verifier fetches that exact path unauthenticated, so it's carved out of
# the .json block by name via negative lookahead rather than moved
# somewhere this rule can't reach, since the path is fixed by the Digital
# Asset Links spec and can't be relocated).
<FilesMatch "^(?!assetlinks\.json$).*\.(kt|java|kts|gradle|py|rb|sh|pl|json|md|txt|lock|dist|conf|pem|key|crt|p12|jks|keystore)$">
    Require all denied
</FilesMatch>

# Dotfiles: .env, .git/, .htpasswd, editor leftovers.
<FilesMatch "^\.">
    Require all denied
</FilesMatch>

# Never list a directory that has no index file.
Options -Indexes

# =============================================================
# ANDROID APP LINKS — friendly per-app paths for password reset
# =============================================================
# The three flavors (customer/rider/vendor) need distinct URL paths, not
# just distinct query strings, because an Android intent-filter's <data>
# element matches on host+path, never on a query parameter. Without this,
# all three apps would have to register the same https://host/reset-password.php
# URL, and if more than one flavor is installed on the same device Android
# shows a disambiguation dialog on every reset link — the exact chooser
# problem the per-app custom schemes were built to avoid.
#
# These rewrite to the existing reset-password.php?scheme=... contract
# (unchanged) so the page itself needed no changes. QSA keeps ?token=...
# intact across the rewrite.
RewriteEngine On
RewriteRule ^go/afamfresh-rider/reset-password/?$ reset-password.php?scheme=afamfresh-rider [L,QSA]
RewriteRule ^go/afamfresh-vendor/reset-password/?$ reset-password.php?scheme=afamfresh-vendor [L,QSA]
RewriteRule ^go/afamfresh/reset-password/?$ reset-password.php?scheme=afamfresh [L,QSA]

# =============================================================
# SESSION COOKIE HARDENING
# =============================================================
# Every one of the ~30 files across api/ and admin/ that starts a session
# calls session_start() itself, most before ever requiring
# admin/includes/config.php -- so a session_set_cookie_params() call placed
# in that shared config file, as would be the natural place, runs too late
# for nearly every request; the cookie has often already been issued by
# then. Apache config is applied before any PHP script runs at all
# (confirmed mod_php: the Dockerfile's base image is php:8.2-apache), so
# this is the one place that reliably applies before every session_start()
# regardless of which file calls it first.
#
# HttpOnly/Secure: irrelevant to the Android app's own HTTP client (it
# doesn't run JS and only ever calls the https:// URL) and irrelevant to a
# same-origin admin page's own requests -- this only ever removes
# capability from an attacker (JS cookie theft, cookie sent over plain
# HTTP), never from a legitimate caller.
# SameSite=Lax: still allows normal top-level navigation/link-following,
# blocks the cross-site POST shape CSRF relies on. Native HTTP clients
# (the Android app) don't apply SameSite restrictions at all, so this is
# free for the app and a real backstop for the browser-based admin panel.
php_flag session.cookie_httponly On
php_flag session.cookie_secure On
php_value session.cookie_samesite Lax
